//? Title: A piecewise linear learned index with exact correction
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Fit local linear predictors of insertion rank on an unevenly spaced integer
key set. Bound the rank errors and verify the resulting lookups against
ordinary binary search at every query in the fixture.
)

> A rank model with a certified correction

For a sorted array, lower bound returns the first index whose key is at
least the query. As the query increases, this rank is a staircase. An
index can fit a line on each equal-width key interval to estimate where
the search should start. Each observed key is paired with its first
occurrence rank, so duplicate keys retain lower-bound semantics.

Datamunge floors the prediction, clips it to the valid rank range, and stores the minimum and maximum signed
errors on each interval. Between keys the true rank is constant and
the floored linear prediction is monotone. The extreme errors can
therefore be found at the endpoints of each constant-rank stretch,
including both sides of key discontinuities. A binary search between
the certified rank bounds returns the exact rank. The upper rank is a
valid insertion position even when it is the end iterator of the search.

> Fit and verify the upstream integer fixture

The program generates the same 1,800 nonlinear keys and 18 duplicates as
the TensorFlow example. It fits twelve intervals of width 5,000 and
checks every integer query from -1 through 60,000.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/learned_index.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
    constexpr int domain = 60000, span = 5000;
    std::vector<std::int32_t> keys;
    for (int i = 0; i < 1800; ++i) {
        const double f = double(i) / 1800;
        keys.push_back(int(domain * (.37 * f + .63 * f * f)));
    }
    for (int j = 0; j < 18; ++j) keys.push_back(keys[j * 83]);
    std::ranges::sort(keys);
    const dm::algorithms::LearnedIndex index(keys, span);
    std::vector<double> x, rank, lo, hi;
    std::size_t widest = 0;
    for (int q = -1; q <= domain; ++q) {
        const auto expected = std::size_t(std::ranges::lower_bound(keys, q) - keys.begin());
        const auto [lower, upper] = index.rank_bounds(q);
        if (index.lower_bound(q) != expected || lower > expected || expected > upper)
            throw std::runtime_error("Certified lookup differs from binary search");
        widest = std::max(widest, upper - lower);
        if (q >= 10000 && q <= 10500) {
            x.push_back(q);
            rank.push_back(expected);
            lo.push_back(lower);
            hi.push_back(upper);
        }
    }
    if (widest >= keys.size() / 10) throw std::runtime_error("Correction window is too wide");
    const auto min = std::numeric_limits<std::int32_t>::min();
    const auto max = std::numeric_limits<std::int32_t>::max();
    const dm::algorithms::LearnedIndex sparse({min, min, max}, 1000000000);
    if (sparse.lower_bound(min) != 0 || sparse.lower_bound(0) != 2 || sparse.lower_bound(max) != 2)
        throw std::runtime_error("Sparse or duplicate boundary case failed");

    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    dm::graphics::RPlot::create().title("Exact ranks within certified search bounds")
        .x_label("Integer query").y_label("Insertion rank")
        .lines(x, lo, "Lower bound", {153, 82, 43})
        .lines(x, hi, "Upper bound", {153, 82, 43})
        .lines(x, rank, "Exact rank", {31, 97, 158})
        .save_svg("build/learned_index.svg");
    std::print("{} keys. All {} queries matched binary search. Widest correction window: {} ranks.\n",
               keys.size(), domain + 2, widest);
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
1818 keys. All 60002 queries matched binary search. Widest correction window: 20 ranks.
\image(figures/learned_index.svg)
// result_end

The figure zooms into a small part of the key space so the rank staircase
and its enclosing bounds are visible. A narrow correction interval is a
property of this fitted fixture. It does not by itself establish a lookup
speedup, which also depends on the cost of locating and evaluating a model.
